202903136610985,0.02805478684604168,0.009841467253863811,0.012667691335082054,0.027662599459290504,-0.01862628012895584,0.012532428838312626,0.003921066876500845,0.007127663586288691,0.01904108375310898,-0.020599184557795525,-0.013828813098371029,-0.004765105899423361,0.002537060296162963,-0.025899507105350494,0.0003851353540085256,-0.01579941250383854,0.002966038417071104,0.020301833748817444,0.03476240113377571,-0.015945591032505035,Masked Modeling for Representation Learning,"Learning rich, contextualized representations (embeddings) from sequential data (like text, genes, or even images) without requiring extensive labeled data.","Self-supervised learning scenarios, particularly with transformer architectures, where the goal is to capture underlying patterns and relationships in the data.","Randomly mask out a portion of the input sequence (e.g., 20% of gene embeddings). A model (e.g., a transformer) is then trained to predict the masked-out elements based on the surrounding context. This forces the model to learn a deep understanding of the data's structure and dependencies.","Produces high-quality, context-aware embeddings that can be used for downstream tasks like classification, clustering, or cross-domain analysis, often generalizing well even with limited labeled data.","Learning embeddings for text (BERT), genes, images, video, etc.",
