.008068076334893703,0.019805748015642166,0.015155084431171417,0.005126940086483955,0.027359478175640106,-0.0026960945688188076,0.016589919105172157,-0.004655981902033091,-0.0021882106084376574,0.0012966596987098455,-0.00574604282155633,-0.009248314425349236,-0.028554977849125862,0.00752126332372427,-0.035867542028427124,-0.01103041972965002,0.004712862428277731,0.00026274353149347007,0.009278727695345879,0.02193428948521614,-0.01534265372902155,Functional Grounding (GLAM Method),"Large Language Models (LLMs) suffer from a lack of grounding, meaning their abstract knowledge (statistical structures from text training) does not align with the dynamics and relational structures of interactive environments. This limits their functional competence and ability to solve decision-making problems.","An agent uses an LLM as a policy in an interactive environment (e.g., textual world, robotics). The LLM's pre-trained knowledge is valuable but needs to be aligned with the specific environment's physics and interaction rules.","Progressively update the LLM's policy as the agent interacts with the environment. This is achieved by leveraging online Reinforcement Learning (RL) to finetune the LLM based on observations and rewards collected from direct interaction. The LLM's language modeling heads are used to compute action probabilities, and a value head is added for RL.","The LLM adapts and learns to solve various tasks (e.g., spatial, navigation) with drastically improved sample efficiency and generalization abilities (to new objects, some new tasks) compared to zero-shot LLMs, supervised finetuning, or RL finetuning of non-pretrained LLMs. It enables the LLM to align its internal symbols with external dynamics.",Training LLM-based agents to perform tasks in interactive environments; improving LLM's understanding of real-world physics and causality; enhancing functional competence beyond text generation.,
