31,-0.0005658799200318754,0.010270903818309307,0.02308153733611107,0.014491017907857895,0.02218175120651722,0.01246249582618475,0.01897846721112728,-0.020574668422341347,0.005432556848973036,-0.002938605612143874,-0.014535510912537575,-0.01489550806581974,-0.022759035229682922,0.016628047451376915,-0.02115430310368538,-0.005922231823205948,0.004916899371892214,0.006352283991873264,0.011407371610403061,0.02710268646478653,-0.01578342355787754,LLM as Policy,"How can Large Language Models (LLMs), primarily trained for text generation, be directly used to control an agent's actions in an interactive environment to achieve goals?","An agent needs to produce actions towards goals in an interactive environment, perceiving the outcome of these actions. The LLM possesses abstract knowledge and reasoning capabilities from its pre-training.","Gather the task description, current observation, and set of possible actions into a prompt for the LLM. The LLM then outputs a probability distribution over these possible actions. This can be done by computing the conditional probability of each token sequence representing an action using the LLM's language modeling heads.","The LLM directly controls the agent's behavior, leveraging its pre-trained knowledge. This allows for the application of LLMs in decision-making tasks beyond pure language generation.","Enabling LLMs to act as decision-makers in interactive environments (textual games, robotics); leveraging LLM's reasoning for action selection.",
