6319785118,-0.005829746834933758,0.015346014872193336,0.01985762268304825,0.017182987183332443,0.02092009223997593,0.0040004923939704895,0.0039912923239171505,-0.00856990460306406,-0.0020753303542733192,-0.002286679344251752,0.005892399698495865,-0.006084802560508251,-0.033895399421453476,0.013115646317601204,-0.02572951465845108,-0.010046691633760929,0.00014615769032388926,0.006130802445113659,0.01744040846824646,0.02250257506966591,-0.00927354022860527,Distributed LLM Policies,"Using very large Large Language Models (LLMs) as agent policies in online Reinforcement Learning (RL) is computationally expensive. Computing action probabilities for multiple possible actions across many parallel environments at each step can become a significant bottleneck, making training intractable.",An online RL setup requires frequent and fast inference from a large LLM to compute action probabilities for multiple parallel environments and potentially many possible actions per environment.,"Deploy multiple LLM workers (e.g., N instances), each handling a subset of actions to score in parallel. Additionally, distribute the training process (gradient computation for minibatches) across these instances and gather gradients before updating the models. A client-server architecture (e.g., Lamorel library) can manage this distribution.","Achieves a quasi-linear time decrease with the number of deployed LLMs, making online RL finetuning of large LLMs computationally feasible and scalable.",Scaling online RL training for large LLM-based agents; overcoming computational bottlenecks in LLM inference and training for interactive tasks.,
