.007904226891696453,0.0162538792937994,0.01109288539737463,0.020926613360643387,0.007840409874916077,0.018293865025043488,-0.008055290207266808,0.003051945473998785,-0.006751229055225849,-0.009157980792224407,-0.019938157871365547,-0.02664547972381115,0.017556266859173775,-0.014515869319438934,0.003293257672339678,0.009595490992069244,0.008043169975280762,0.01632862165570259,0.018767429515719414,-0.018005533143877983,Prompt-Based Action Probability Calculation,"When using an LLM as a policy, how can it output a probability distribution over a discrete set of possible actions, especially when the actions are natural language phrases, without requiring ad-hoc mapping or new, un-pretrained network heads?","An LLM is used as an agent's policy in an interactive environment. The environment provides a set of possible actions, each represented as a sequence of tokens (natural language). The LLM needs to select an action based on the current prompt (goal + observation).","For each possible action, compute its conditional probability given the prompt by leveraging the LLM's inherent language modeling capabilities. This involves calculating the product of conditional probabilities of each token in the action sequence, given the prompt and preceding tokens of the action. These log probabilities are then normalized using a softmax function to obtain a distribution over all possible actions.","The LLM can directly output a probability distribution over natural language actions using its pre-trained operations, avoiding the need for ad-hoc mappings or additional, randomly initialized action heads. This method is robust to any textual action space.",Enabling LLMs to select actions from a predefined set of natural language commands; leveraging LLM's language modeling heads for policy output; maintaining robustness to varying action spaces.,
