,-0.014316658489406109,0.013278431259095669,0.018792491406202316,0.006625030655413866,0.02307835966348648,-0.001963625429198146,0.018221797421574593,-0.006619543768465519,0.006911085452884436,0.002252803184092045,-0.009381976909935474,-0.007881232537329197,-0.024010509252548218,0.017141908407211304,-0.018076015636324883,-0.008419833146035671,0.017114799469709396,0.0035426367539912462,-0.006489121820777655,0.018200844526290894,-0.006543579511344433,Generalization to Out-of-Vocabulary/Invented Symbols,"How can an LLM-based agent maintain functional competence and generalize its learned skills when encountering new, unseen, or even invented symbols (nouns, adjectives) in task descriptions, without explicit re-training for these new symbols?",An LLM-based agent has been functionally grounded in an environment for a set of tasks. New tasks or environments introduce objects or properties described by words not present in the training vocabulary or by entirely invented words.,"Leverage the LLM's pre-trained knowledge and its functional grounding. The LLM's ability to map words to an embedding space, even for unseen words, allows it to infer relationships and properties based on context and its vast pre-training. The functional grounding ensures it focuses on the geometry and actions rather than specific object identities.","The LLM-based agent can retain strong performance even with out-of-vocabulary nouns or invented objects, indicating that it has functionally grounded the underlying concepts (e.g., geometry, actions) rather than just memorizing specific symbol-action mappings. Performance might decrease slightly for entirely invented words but remains significantly better than baselines.",Building robust LLM agents that can handle novel entities in their environment; reducing the need for constant re-training when new objects or descriptions are introduced.,
