3560731411,-0.01738978736102581,0.009862363338470459,0.0242592953145504,0.003085491945967078,0.015817120671272278,-0.004437406547367573,0.014829883351922035,-0.008123507723212242,0.003166650654748082,-0.006050796248018742,-0.011746386997401714,-0.02084510587155819,-0.020141933113336563,0.013060970231890678,-0.03137565404176712,-0.005282008089125156,0.0173635371029377,0.005816807504743338,-0.0034076550509780645,0.015783244743943214,-0.02847774513065815,Multilingual Generalization (Limited),"Can an LLM-based agent, functionally grounded in one language, transfer its learned skills and understanding to tasks described in a different language without explicit multilingual training for the interactive task?","An LLM (e.g., FlanT5) has been pre-trained on a multilingual corpus and then functionally grounded in an interactive environment using instructions in one language (e.g., English). The agent is then presented with tasks in a different language (e.g., French).",Rely on the LLM's inherent multilingual capabilities from its pre-training. The LLM might have learned to map words across languages to similar embedding spaces.,"Generalization to a completely new language for the entire environment (observations, goals, actions) is very poor, often worse than random. However, partial generalization (e.g., only actions in a new language) shows slightly better, though still significantly degraded, performance. This suggests that when too many grounded symbols are modified at once, functional grounding fails to transfer effectively.","Understanding the limitations of cross-lingual transfer for functionally grounded LLM agents; informing strategies for multilingual AI agent development (e.g., requiring multilingual grounding or more targeted translation layers).",
