18106138333678246,0.015941178426146507,0.01607309654355049,0.02846154011785984,-0.003339522983878851,0.0052321963012218475,-0.005576159805059433,0.005899761337786913,-0.001654819818213582,-0.016124160960316658,-0.029178934171795845,-0.02272924967110157,-0.0021458875853568316,-0.03498339280486107,-0.00551787531003356,0.00553119508549571,0.0012288270518183708,0.013295559212565422,0.03400227800011635,-0.01694808527827263,Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG),"Given a task, how can agents conduct reasoning with data/knowledge that are not learned by the foundation models through model training, while addressing lack of knowledge, computational overhead, and data privacy concerns.","Large foundational model-based agents are not equipped with knowledge related to explicitly specific domains, especially on highly confidential and privacy-sensitive local data, unless finetuned or pretrained using domain data.","Enhances the accuracy and reliability of agents with facts retrieved from external sources (internal or online data). It fills knowledge gaps using parameterized knowledge (e.g., vector databases) without pretraining or finetuning the model, preserving data privacy and reducing costs. The retrieved local data is sent back to the agent via prompts for in-context learning.","{'Benefits': 'Knowledge retrieval, Updatability, Data privacy, Cost-efficiency.', 'Drawbacks': 'Maintenance overhead (of parameterized knowledge), Data limitation (agent still relies on its training for generation quality).'}",LinkedIn applies RAG for case studies; Yan et al. (2024b) devise a retrieval evaluator for RAG; Levonian et al. (2023) apply RAG with GPT-3.5 for math question-answering.,
