9471188188,0.015782782807946205,0.00734435161575675,0.020488156005740166,0.0413442924618721,0.014798661693930626,-0.0037945187650620937,-0.01973397098481655,-0.0029096051584929228,-0.00044135484495200217,-0.003160236170515418,-0.009634390473365784,-0.03625376895070076,0.024990372359752655,-0.0067678941413760185,-0.008378912694752216,0.007261093705892563,-0.006935948505997658,0.002335429424419999,0.022518545389175415,-0.015678003430366516,MasterSlave,"A master agent needs to delegate a task to another agent, often for parallel execution, remote execution, or to maintain a local presence (e.g., GUI) while the task is performed elsewhere.","An agent has multiple responsibilities and needs to offload a task for parallel processing, or a stationary agent (e.g., managing a GUI) needs to perform a task at a remote destination without losing its local state.","A master agent creates a slave agent and delegates a task to it. The slave agent moves to a destination, performs the assigned task, and returns with the result. The pattern uses abstract Master and Slave classes to localize invariant parts of delegation, dispatching, task execution, and exception handling, allowing subclasses to implement variable aspects.","Enables parallel task execution, allows stationary agents to perform remote tasks, and simplifies agent design and implementation by promoting code reuse. While the initial implementation might fix slave behavior at design time, a delegation-based model can offer more flexibility.",Performing a task in parallel with other responsibilities; a stationary agent maintaining a GUI while a slave performs a remote task and reports results; searching for files in a remote archive.,
