2233,0.01566186547279358,0.018002329394221306,0.01924094930291176,0.0032392768189311028,0.006106940563768148,-0.007469725329428911,-0.0010840017348527908,0.006246316246688366,0.0020824086386710405,-0.033446259796619415,-0.016700325533747673,0.0031103857327252626,-0.006451886612921953,-0.008975321426987648,0.003105253679677844,-0.00391260115429759,-8.204495679819956e-05,0.026218488812446594,-0.016632836312055588,Context-Aware Algorithm Selection,"How to choose the most appropriate Machine Learning (ML) algorithm given various operational and environmental constraints, such as user preferences, data characteristics, and specific model configuration requirements.","Developing an ML solution where the applicability and optimal configuration of algorithms are highly dependent on the specific context of use, including the end-user's needs, the properties of the input data, and the algorithm's own parameter sensitivities.","Define and monitor User Contexts (user preferences), Data Contexts (dataset characteristics), and Model Contexts (algorithm configuration requirements). Link these contexts to specific ML algorithms or algorithm families via 'Means-End' links, indicating when an algorithm is applicable or how it should be configured under certain conditions. A 'Context Analyzer' component can systematically discover alternatives based on which contexts hold true.","Ensures that the selected ML algorithm is suitable for the specific operational environment, data properties, and user expectations, leading to more effective, robust, and contextually appropriate ML solutions. It reduces trial-and-error in algorithm selection and configuration.","Illustrated in the Loan Approval pattern where kNearest Neighbor is applicable when 'Users desire simplicity', Naive Bayes when 'Features are independent', and Support Vector Machines require 'Decrease parameter C when dataset is noisy'. Also used in Fraud Detection to determine applicable anomaly detection algorithms.",
