4,-0.00029299125890247524,0.02438848651945591,0.016452327370643616,0.019490418955683708,0.01653362810611725,-0.005015092436224222,-0.0012053288519382477,0.007143924944102764,-0.001733814598992467,0.010882072150707245,-0.010656511411070824,-0.02302589826285839,-0.013450340367853642,-0.005355963483452797,-0.01765666902065277,-0.01111390721052885,-0.006038829684257507,-0.007510643918067217,0.022660011425614357,0.011077574454247952,-0.011930999346077442,Supervised/Semi-Supervised Anomaly Detection Strategy,How to choose the appropriate anomaly detection approach (supervised vs. semi-supervised) based on the availability and labeling of anomalous data samples during training.,"Implementing an anomaly detection system (e.g., for fraud detection) where the nature of available training data—specifically, whether labeled examples of both normal and anomalous instances are present—dictates the feasible and most effective learning paradigm.","If both fraud (anomalous) and non-fraud (normal) samples are available and labeled, employ a supervised anomaly detection approach. If only examples of non-fraud claims (normal data) are present, and anomalous data is scarce or unlabeled, utilize a semi-supervised anomaly detection approach.","Selects the most effective and appropriate anomaly detection method given the constraints of the training data, leading to a more accurate, robust, and practical fraud or anomaly detection system.","In the fraud detection project, the team realized that 'depending on the data availability some anomaly detection approaches might be more suitable than others. In particular if both fraud and nonfraud samples are available a supervised anomaly detection approach should be used but if only examples of nonfraud claims were present a semisupervised approach is appropriate'.",
