043940264731645584,0.007130295503884554,0.010000854730606079,0.03197035565972328,0.006956941913813353,0.01583613082766533,0.020096430554986,0.014235339127480984,0.012564585544168949,-0.008237982168793678,-0.026223793625831604,-0.01468805130571127,0.009886888787150383,0.006803281605243683,-0.007464210037142038,-0.011183690279722214,0.007346814498305321,0.009985444135963917,0.03501255065202713,-0.015739135444164276,Neutral Class,"In classification problems, some examples have labels that are essentially arbitrary, ambiguous, or where human experts disagree, leading to poor model accuracy if forced into binary or strict multiclass categories.","Classification problems where a subset of training examples falls into an 'in-between' or 'undecided' category, or where the distinction between classes is subjective (e.g., Apgar scores, pain reliever choice, customer satisfaction).","Introduce an additional 'neutral' label (e.g., 'Maybe', 'Neutral') for the classification model, disjoint from the primary classes. This requires appropriate data collection or labeling design.","Improves model accuracy by not forcing the model to learn arbitrary distinctions, increases user trust (by allowing 'neutral' determinations), and helps disambiguate situations of insufficient data from genuine expert disagreement.","Pain reliever guidance (ibuprofen, acetaminophen, neutral), Apgar score prediction (healthy, needs attention, neutral), customer satisfaction (good, bad, neutral), flight pricing (buy, don't buy, neutral).",
