811,0.015053723938763142,0.018190966919064522,-0.014180661179125309,0.032295893877744675,-0.007595626171678305,0.02567710354924202,0.005270469933748245,-0.021065281704068184,0.007666151039302349,0.0006429993081837893,-0.01801449991762638,-0.01864147186279297,0.002966874046251178,-0.005028000567108393,-0.009837936609983444,-0.010058767162263393,-0.006264010909944773,0.02286789007484913,0.02835104428231716,-0.006528100930154324,Useful Overfitting,"Traditional machine learning emphasizes avoiding overfitting to ensure generalization, but in certain scenarios (e.g., approximating a known physical system), perfect fit to the training data is the goal.","Situations where the entire input space can be exhaustively tabulated, and a precise, deterministic solution (e.g., from a physics-based model or PDE) is available for all inputs, with no noise or statistical variability. Also useful for knowledge distillation or sanity-checking model code.","Intentionally overfit on the training dataset by forgoing usual generalization mechanisms like early stopping, regularization, or validation datasets. The goal is to make the ML model approximate the precise solution as perfectly as possible.","Creates an ML model that acts as a fast approximation to a complex, deterministic system, providing rapid inference. Also serves as a sanity check during model development to ensure the model has enough capacity and the training setup is correct.","Simulating physical/dynamical systems (e.g., radiative transfer models, PDEs), knowledge distillation from large to small models, troubleshooting neural network code.",
