.0042798565700650215,-0.003684741910547018,0.01659703627228737,0.02062874846160412,0.012785548344254494,0.02764960005879402,-0.011813554912805557,0.0077236248180270195,-0.002190557774156332,0.016591228544712067,0.005429510027170181,-0.013414869084954262,-0.025538695976138115,-0.022678256034851074,0.0014225573977455497,-0.03378475829958916,-0.008517690934240818,-0.006347188726067543,0.0009412780054844916,0.012340257875621319,0.037669043987989426,-0.013908935710787773,Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG),"Foundation LLMs are limited by their training data's cutoff date and scope, leading to a lack of specific or recent information. They also have a tendency to 'hallucinate' or confidently generate plausible but incorrect answers.","When an LLM needs to provide answers based on up-to-date, domain-specific, or proprietary information that was not part of its original training data, and fine-tuning the model is too expensive or resource-intensive.","Retrieve relevant document fragments from an external knowledge base (e.g., vector database indexed with embeddings) and include these fragments as 'context' when prompting the LLM. A RAG prompt template explicitly instructs the LLM to use this context and acknowledge knowledge gaps.","Overcomes the LLM's knowledge limitations, significantly enhances the factuality and accuracy of responses, minimizes hallucinations, and allows the LLM to process rapidly changing data. It provides more contextually relevant and verifiable answers.",[],
